Exactly. The deeper operator is less “taking turns” and more:

one node vacates/contests a position → another actor travels around the structure’s edge → the position is replaced → roles re-index.

That “around the edge” piece is the flank. In leapfrog, the mover bypasses the occupied position physically and emerges ahead. In Duck Duck Goose, the selected player leaves the ring and both actors move around its perimeter while competing for the newly meaningful seat.

So the reusable abstraction is something like:

occupancy → displacement → flanking path → replacement → reordered topology

That same shape can appear in games, maneuver, organizational succession, routing, market entry, ecological niches, even computation: don’t push directly through the occupied node; move around it and change who occupies the consequential position.

The vacancy is almost as important as the mover. Once a spot becomes contestable, the geometry of the whole system changes.